   "subject": "Reverse rainbow color scale from scale_colour_gradientn",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>I am trying to plot water temperature data, symbolized using scale_colour_gradientn(colours = rainbow(10)). The plot is perfect, except that the cool temperatures are red and warm temperatures are violet (opposite of what&#39;s intuitive). </div><div><br></div><div>Is there an argument that will flip the colors, or is another palette that is better to use for temperature data?</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "LTqTACAJ1L0J",
   "from": "baptiste auguie",
   "author": "a3c668b849637905",
   "date": "2013-07-12T13:07:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: Reverse rainbow color scale from scale_colour_gradientn",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "colours = rev(rainbow(10)) ?<div><br></div><div>HTH,</div><div><br></div><div>b.<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 12 July 2013 01:25, Kevin Stierhoff <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">kevin.s...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div>I am trying to plot water temperature data, symbolized using scale_colour_gradientn(colours = rainbow(10)). The plot is perfect, except that the cool temperatures are red and warm temperatures are violet (opposite of what&#39;s intuitive). </div>\r\n<div><br></div><div>Is there an argument that will flip the colors, or is another palette that is better to use for temperature data?</div></blockquote></div></div>"
